      "conf": "VERIFIED · PRIMARY & WIRE",
      "dek": "The US government borrowed for seven years today at 5.085%, the highest rate it has paid on that loan since 1993, and had to go slightly above the price the market was quoting to get the sale away. Longer-term interest rates followed: the ten-year rose to 5.18% from 5.11% and the thirty-year to 5.47%, while the two-year barely moved, so this is about the cost of lending money for a long time rather than about what the Federal Reserve does next. Oil added 2.82%. Yet the S&P 500 closed at 7,704.13, down 0.02%. Higher long rates quietly raise the bar every company has to clear; watch the ten-year into next week.",
      "headline": "Borrowing costs hit a 33-year high and shares barely moved",
