      "conf": "VERIFIED · WIRE & PRIMARY",
      "dek": "When the US government sells debt, foreign central banks and other indirect buyers usually absorb most of it. Today they took 57.2% of a roughly $44 billion seven-year sale, down from about 61% last month, and the notes had to clear 0.7 of a basis point above the yield the market was quoting. Cover fell to 2.42 times the amount offered from 2.50. This is not a failed auction; it is buyers asking for a better price to hold the same paper. It matters because on the same afternoon the Federal Reserve's own survey of large dealers described financing conditions as basically unchanged.",
      "headline": "Foreign buyers step back from a big US government debt sale",
